            <div xml:id="d211">

                <pb facs="0280" n="272" sameAs="frus:frus1941v03/pg272"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/10483: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Egypt</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Kirk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-04-29T13:00:00+03:00">April 29, 1941—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 30—9:07 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>364. My 363, April 29, noon.<anchor n="15" xml:id="d211-f1"/> Although the
                    advance into Egypt is reported to have halted a short distance over the frontier
                    I understand that it has already served to give the German and Italian forces
                    control of two important advance airfields and the passes down the escarpment at
                    Halfaya and Sollum.</p>
                <p>In the course of my latest conversations with the highest military authorities
                    here the comment has been only general and to the effect that critical times are
                    ahead.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
